# Copy to local.mk and edit. Ignored by git, so what you put in it
# stays out of the repository.
#
# You most likely do not need it. With one codesigning certificate in
# your keychain the build finds it and signs with it, and there is
# nothing to configure. Name one here in two cases:
#
#   - you have more than one, and the build refuses to choose;
#   - yours is not reported as valid by the keychain, which is true
#     of a self-signed certificate.
#
# If you have no certificate at all, the build signs ad-hoc, and the
# Accessibility grant resets on every rebuild. Getting one takes a
# minute and a free Apple ID is enough: Xcode > Settings > Apple
# Accounts > your Apple ID > Manage Certificates... > + > Apple
# Development.
#
# Ask the keychain for the exact string rather than typing it out:
#
#     $ security find-identity -v -p codesigning
#       1) 5A0F... "Apple Development: Your Name (XXXXXXXXXX)"
#          1 valid identities found
#
# and put what is inside the quotes here, without the quotes:

# CODESIGN_IDENTITY = Apple Development: Your Name (XXXXXXXXXX)

# Where make install puts the bundle. The default is ~/Applications.
# A system-wide path makes install use sudo.

# INSTALL_DIR = /Applications
